1
00:00:00,547 --> 00:00:02,475
Turn one face of a Rubik's cube.

2
00:00:02,756 --> 00:00:03,434
Now another.

3
00:00:03,775 --> 00:00:07,779
Fifty four colored stickers seem to demand fifty four little records.

4
00:00:09,032 --> 00:00:09,967
But look closer.

5
00:00:10,309 --> 00:00:12,250
The stickers never travel alone.

6
00:00:12,578 --> 00:00:14,968
They ride on small, rigid cubes.

7
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:21,650
Could we remember the geometry of those cubes, and let the colors take care of themselves?

8
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:25,240
Rubix does exactly that.

9
00:00:25,529 --> 00:00:29,782
And the surprising part is how much information one tiny home address contains.

10
00:00:31,693 --> 00:00:34,169
We will follow this green and white cubelet.

11
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:36,353
An edge, with two stickers.

12
00:00:37,496 --> 00:00:40,449
First, find an address that identifies it.

13
00:00:40,801 --> 00:00:45,046
Second, discover how that address already knows its colors.

14
00:00:45,426 --> 00:00:49,032
Third, turn that description into a moving cube.

15
00:00:50,526 --> 00:00:56,389
By the end, we will need just two things for each cubelet: its home address, and one rotation.

16
00:00:56,677 --> 00:00:57,416
Let's see why.

17
00:00:59,146 --> 00:01:01,203
Put the origin at the middle of the puzzle.

18
00:01:01,496 --> 00:01:04,303
Replace each cubelet by a point at its center.

19
00:01:05,364 --> 00:01:11,346
Along each axis, there are only three levels: minus one, zero, and one.

20
00:01:11,684 --> 00:01:12,971
Twenty seven points.

21
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:17,984
The hidden origin has no stickers, so Rubix stores the other twenty six.

22
00:01:19,931 --> 00:01:22,033
We also need directions that stay put.

23
00:01:22,365 --> 00:01:29,134
The six centers give us exactly that: outer face turns spin them in place without changing their positions.

24
00:01:30,315 --> 00:01:35,946
Choose green as positive x, red as positive y, and white as positive z.

25
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:39,625
The opposite faces define the negative directions.

26
00:01:40,931 --> 00:01:44,078
The cube's own anatomy has handed us a coordinate system.

27
00:01:46,092 --> 00:01:51,456
Our green and white edge belongs one step toward green and one step toward white.

28
00:01:51,811 --> 00:01:55,422
Its home coordinate is one, zero, one.

29
00:01:55,776 --> 00:01:56,471
Call it c.

30
00:01:57,773 --> 00:01:59,143
Now freeze that address.

31
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:05,814
The cubelet can travel anywhere, but c keeps saying which cubelet it is, and where it belongs.

32
00:02:07,849 --> 00:02:10,294
Before going further, test this choice.

33
00:02:10,605 --> 00:02:16,047
A nonzero coordinate means a cubelet touches an outer boundary along that axis.

34
00:02:17,261 --> 00:02:19,370
One such coordinate gives a center.

35
00:02:19,733 --> 00:02:20,677
Two give an edge.

36
00:02:21,054 --> 00:02:22,026
Three give a corner.

37
00:02:23,216 --> 00:02:26,252
Add the absolute values, and you have counted its stickers.

38
00:02:26,549 --> 00:02:28,912
The address already knows the cubelet type!

39
00:02:31,235 --> 00:02:34,921
That answers our first question: how to identify the cubelet.

40
00:02:35,235 --> 00:02:36,171
Now for the second.

41
00:02:36,485 --> 00:02:37,750
We still need its colors.

42
00:02:38,945 --> 00:02:41,303
Look again at one, zero, one.

43
00:02:41,663 --> 00:02:45,242
Could the answer already be sitting inside those three numbers?

44
00:02:48,144 --> 00:02:51,639
At home, a green sticker points toward the green center.

45
00:02:51,978 --> 00:02:54,223
A white sticker points toward the white center.

46
00:02:55,327 --> 00:02:58,146
Draw an arrow perpendicular to each sticker.

47
00:02:58,495 --> 00:03:02,095
These outward normals are positive x and positive z.

48
00:03:03,509 --> 00:03:06,009
We can identify a color by its home direction.

49
00:03:06,341 --> 00:03:09,866
That identity stays fixed, even when the sticker turns.

50
00:03:11,816 --> 00:03:15,367
Now split the home address into its axis components.

51
00:03:15,741 --> 00:03:22,489
One, zero, one becomes one x arrow, no y arrow, and one z arrow.

52
00:03:24,097 --> 00:03:24,703
There they are.

53
00:03:24,995 --> 00:03:26,924
The green normal, and the white normal!

54
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:33,700
The coordinate that located our cubelet also contained the directions of its stickers.

55
00:03:34,019 --> 00:03:38,540
Negative components work the same way: minus x identifies blue.

56
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:41,860
There is just one adjustment.

57
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:45,459
Adding the arrows hides their individual identities.

58
00:03:45,806 --> 00:03:48,165
So keep them side by side, as columns.

59
00:03:49,474 --> 00:03:51,960
That gives the diagonal matrix of c.

60
00:03:52,390 --> 00:03:56,528
For our edge, its diagonal is one, zero, one.

61
00:03:56,899 --> 00:03:57,436
Green.

62
00:03:57,807 --> 00:03:58,805
An empty column.

63
00:03:59,191 --> 00:03:59,638
White.

64
00:04:01,281 --> 00:04:05,796
The zero is doing useful work: it says there is no sticker on that axis.

65
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:09,756
A corner has three nonzero columns; a center has one.

66
00:04:11,683 --> 00:04:13,846
Remember how the address counted stickers?

67
00:04:14,183 --> 00:04:16,964
The diagonal matrix tells the same story.

68
00:04:18,055 --> 00:04:21,271
Its nonzero columns point along different axes.

69
00:04:21,631 --> 00:04:24,745
Counting those independent columns gives its rank.

70
00:04:25,105 --> 00:04:27,318
So rank equals the sticker count.

71
00:04:28,646 --> 00:04:30,685
This is why the encoding feels elegant.

72
00:04:30,986 --> 00:04:36,679
The physical boundaries, the cubelet type, and the color directions all agree, because they came from the

73
00:04:36,679 --> 00:04:37,690
same geometry.

74
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:41,179
Two discoveries down.

75
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,895
The home address names the cubelet, and its components identify the stickers.

76
00:04:47,059 --> 00:04:48,693
Our final question is motion.

77
00:04:49,032 --> 00:04:54,304
How can one description carry both the cubelet and its stickers through every turn?

78
00:04:56,559 --> 00:04:59,233
This is where linear algebra earns its place.

79
00:04:59,548 --> 00:05:03,486
A rotation matrix tells us where three basis arrows land.

80
00:05:04,698 --> 00:05:09,576
Once we know their destinations, we can transform any combination of them.

81
00:05:09,925 --> 00:05:12,772
Every vector follows from those three arrows.

82
00:05:14,237 --> 00:05:19,252
Think of the matrix as a motion you can apply, rather than a grid you must memorize.

83
00:05:21,103 --> 00:05:22,848
Let's watch a concrete top turn.

84
00:05:23,172 --> 00:05:23,945
Up stays up.

85
00:05:24,297 --> 00:05:28,973
The x direction swings toward minus y, while y swings toward x.

86
00:05:30,292 --> 00:05:36,240
Our edge travels from one, zero, one to zero, minus one, one.

87
00:05:37,961 --> 00:05:39,818
Its home address is unchanged.

88
00:05:40,145 --> 00:05:42,607
The turn has changed what happens to that address.

89
00:05:44,582 --> 00:05:47,496
Store the accumulated rotation as R.

90
00:05:47,891 --> 00:05:52,526
Apply it to the home vector c, and you get the current position, p.

91
00:05:53,936 --> 00:05:56,089
At the start, R is the identity.

92
00:05:56,422 --> 00:06:00,732
After the turn, R carries that same home address to the new location.

93
00:06:01,825 --> 00:06:03,579
We have recovered where the cubelet is.

94
00:06:03,867 --> 00:06:06,227
Now use the very same motion on its stickers.

95
00:06:07,994 --> 00:06:11,708
The sticker normals are the columns of the diagonal matrix.

96
00:06:12,057 --> 00:06:15,543
Multiplication transforms each column separately.

97
00:06:16,751 --> 00:06:17,525
Rotate them.

98
00:06:17,885 --> 00:06:20,040
Green now points toward minus y.

99
00:06:20,327 --> 00:06:21,595
White still points up.

100
00:06:21,969 --> 00:06:23,746
The empty column stays empty.

101
00:06:25,347 --> 00:06:29,097
One multiplication gives every sticker direction at once!

102
00:06:31,398 --> 00:06:32,423
Now pause here.

103
00:06:32,778 --> 00:06:36,490
This is the connection that ties the whole encoding together.

104
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:41,196
Add the home sticker normals, and you get c.

105
00:06:41,664 --> 00:06:46,001
Rotate those normals, then add them, and you get R times c.

106
00:06:47,839 --> 00:06:49,394
That is the cubelet's position.

107
00:06:51,797 --> 00:06:55,484
So the directions of its stickers already determine where it sits.

108
00:06:55,794 --> 00:06:59,260
One rotation keeps location and facing in agreement.

109
00:06:59,583 --> 00:07:00,836
That is the real payoff.

110
00:07:03,467 --> 00:07:04,624
We can describe motion.

111
00:07:04,917 --> 00:07:07,087
Now we must choose which cubelets receive it.

112
00:07:07,379 --> 00:07:12,112
Turning a face rotates the outer layer behind it: nine cubelets together.

113
00:07:13,183 --> 00:07:14,873
Let v point out of that face.

114
00:07:15,247 --> 00:07:19,291
The dot product with p measures position along that direction.

115
00:07:19,651 --> 00:07:22,940
Positive means the cubelet is in that outer layer.

116
00:07:24,466 --> 00:07:26,963
For the top, that is simply z equals one.

117
00:07:27,251 --> 00:07:30,044
The geometry selects exactly the layer we wanted.

118
00:07:32,043 --> 00:07:34,004
Here is a useful check on our story.

119
00:07:34,328 --> 00:07:38,507
Our edge started on the front, but the top turn carried it to the left.

120
00:07:39,687 --> 00:07:41,948
Its home address still identifies it.

121
00:07:42,302 --> 00:07:47,120
To choose the moving layer, use its current address: R times c.

122
00:07:48,645 --> 00:07:51,978
The two jobs are different, and the notation keeps them clear.

123
00:07:53,733 --> 00:07:55,873
Suppose the next turn is M.

124
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:59,696
Apply the old rotation first, then the new turn.

125
00:08:00,070 --> 00:08:01,498
That gives M times R.

126
00:08:03,005 --> 00:08:04,870
Update the selected cubelets.

127
00:08:05,179 --> 00:08:09,101
Their past moves collapse into one accumulated transformation.

128
00:08:09,411 --> 00:08:12,018
We can forget the sequence and keep its effect.

129
00:08:14,061 --> 00:08:17,417
The implementation follows the same three steps.

130
00:08:17,756 --> 00:08:19,362
Recover the current position.

131
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:22,139
Use the dot product to select the layer.

132
00:08:22,478 --> 00:08:26,320
Multiply the stored rotation by the new move on the left.

133
00:08:27,708 --> 00:08:32,718
Do this for all twenty six home address and rotation pairs, and you have the complete move rule.

134
00:08:34,559 --> 00:08:39,122
Our animation is smooth, but a stored move is exactly a quarter turn.

135
00:08:39,450 --> 00:08:43,075
It only swaps coordinate axes and reverses signs.

136
00:08:44,270 --> 00:08:48,867
So every matrix entry stays minus one, zero, or one.

137
00:08:49,227 --> 00:08:54,521
These matrices remain proper rotations; there are twenty four possibilities.

138
00:08:55,932 --> 00:08:59,618
We get geometric motion with exact arithmetic at the endpoints.

139
00:09:01,450 --> 00:09:02,862
There is one final question.

140
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:04,667
When is a cubelet solved?

141
00:09:06,370 --> 00:09:09,261
When every sticker points in its home direction.

142
00:09:09,653 --> 00:09:14,155
The transformed diagonal matrix must equal the original one.

143
00:09:16,287 --> 00:09:17,458
Remember the column sum?

144
00:09:17,777 --> 00:09:20,742
Equal sticker normals also put the cubelet at home.

145
00:09:21,075 --> 00:09:24,492
The same equality checks facing and location together.

146
00:09:26,852 --> 00:09:29,197
And this condition has a lovely subtlety.

147
00:09:29,530 --> 00:09:30,955
Watch the white center spin.

148
00:09:32,445 --> 00:09:37,536
Its local x and y arrows turn, but the white normal still points up.

149
00:09:37,904 --> 00:09:42,967
Its one sticker is home, so an ordinary cube considers this center solved.

150
00:09:44,392 --> 00:09:50,117
Demanding the identity matrix would ask for extra information the unmarked sticker cannot show.

151
00:09:50,432 --> 00:09:53,907
A picture cube would need that extra orientation constraint.

152
00:09:56,006 --> 00:09:59,723
Four quarter turns bring every cubelet and sticker back.

153
00:10:00,061 --> 00:10:02,709
A turn followed by its inverse cancels too.

154
00:10:04,113 --> 00:10:06,812
These identities are useful tests of the model.

155
00:10:07,126 --> 00:10:11,299
More importantly, they let us watch the geometry close its own loop.

156
00:10:13,769 --> 00:10:15,709
Return to the question we started with.

157
00:10:16,047 --> 00:10:17,973
What does a computer need to remember?

158
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:21,645
A home address, and one rotation.

159
00:10:21,994 --> 00:10:26,376
The address identifies the cubelet and supplies its colors.

160
00:10:26,738 --> 00:10:30,424
The rotation tells us where it is and where its stickers point.

161
00:10:32,022 --> 00:10:36,244
Selecting a layer and applying a turn now follow from that same geometry.

162
00:10:36,567 --> 00:10:42,872
The search for a solution is a separate challenge; this is the small, consistent world it searches.

163
00:10:44,024 --> 00:10:49,181
That is Rubix's clever choice: find a description in which the facts you need emerge together.

164
00:10:52,014 --> 00:10:58,619
This way of seeing matrices was inspired by Grant Sanderson's Essence of Linear Algebra, from Three Blue One

165
00:10:58,619 --> 00:10:59,147
Brown.

166
00:11:00,218 --> 00:11:01,552
It is a wonderful series.

167
00:11:01,836 --> 00:11:06,417
Grant makes the geometry feel discoverable, and the equations feel earned.

168
00:11:06,701 --> 00:11:09,407
If you enjoyed this connection, I warmly recommend it.

169
00:11:10,748 --> 00:11:11,706
Look at the motion.

170
00:11:12,111 --> 00:11:13,368
Find the right description.

171
00:11:13,716 --> 00:11:16,194
Let the mathematics reveal what follows.
